The `enablePrerenderSourceMaps` option lets Node.js use existing source maps during the prerender phase of `next build`. When source maps are available, error stack traces point to the original source code. It defaults to `true`.

This option does not generate source maps. Webpack does not generate server source maps by default. To generate them when investigating prerender errors, use [`next build --debug-prerender`](/docs/app/api-reference/cli/next#debugging-prerender-errors).

To disable it, set `enablePrerenderSourceMaps` to `false`:

```ts filename="next.config.ts" switcher highlight={4}

const nextConfig: NextConfig = {
  enablePrerenderSourceMaps: false,
}

export default nextConfig
```

```js filename="next.config.js" switcher highlight={3}
/** @type {import('next').NextConfig} */
const nextConfig = {
  enablePrerenderSourceMaps: false,
}

module.exports = nextConfig
```

## Reference

| Option                      | Type      | Default |
| --------------------------- | --------- | ------- |
| `enablePrerenderSourceMaps` | `boolean` | `true`  |

Disabling source maps during prerendering can reduce [build memory usage](/docs/app/guides/memory-usage#disable-source-maps), but makes prerendering errors harder to debug.

